  "content": "deterministic cleanup is a critical component of maintaining a stable and efficient inference service. when serving machine learning models, resources like gpu memory, cpu cycles, and network sockets are finite and expensive. if a service fails to release these resources predictably after a request is processed, it leads to memory leaks and fragmentation. over time, these leaks accumulate, causing the service to slow down or crash entirely due to out of memory errors. deterministic cleanup ensures that every allocated object is destroyed at a specific, predictable point in the execution lifecycle. this prevents the accumulation of stale tensors and prevents the buildup of zombie processes that can clog the system. by guaranteeing that resources are freed immediately after use, developers can achieve higher throughput, lower latency, and much greater reliability. without this precision, scaling an inference service becomes impossible because the underlying infrastructure becomes increasingly unstable and unpredictable under heavy load. end",
